sop help
Command-line tools for working with a log of standard operating procedures
(SOPs).  Each SOP is the steps of a routine task, such as rolling back a
release, as an ordered list, so that it is done the same way each time.
Problem playbooks, kept with the plm tool, refer to SOPs for their routine
steps.

SOPs are stored as Markdown files in the project's `docs/sop` directory.
Each SOP's file name begins with `SOP-` and its number, such as
`SOP-0003-roll-back-a-release.md`.  Run "sop init" to start a log, and
"sop new" to add to it.

Usage:
  sop [flags]
  sop [command]

Available Commands:
  completion  Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
  config      Print the configuration in use
  graph       Draw the links between records in Mermaid or Graphviz format
  help        Help about any command
  init        Start a log of SOPs
  link        Record that one SOP supersedes or updates another
  list        List the SOPs, or write a table of contents
  new         Create the next numbered SOP

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for sop

Use "sop [command] --help" for more information about a command.
sop help new
Creates a new, numbered standard operating procedure (SOP), in draft.  The
title is a single argument, so it must be quoted if it contains spaces.  The
SOP is opened for editing in the editor specified by the VISUAL or EDITOR
environment variable (VISUAL is preferred; EDITOR is used if VISUAL is not
set).  After editing, the file name of the SOP is output to stdout, so the
command can be used in scripts.

If the SOP directory contains a file `templates/template.md`, this is used as
the template for the new SOP.  Otherwise a built-in template is used: a
title, a date, a status, and the steps, as an ordered list.

Multiple --supersede and --update options can be given, so that the new SOP
can supersede or update multiple existing SOPs.  A superseded SOP loses its
Draft or Approved status; an updated one keeps its status.

Usage:
  sop new TITLE [flags]

Examples:
  # Create a new SOP with the title "Roll back a release":
  sop new "Roll back a release"

  # Create a new SOP that supersedes SOP 4, after the deployment changed:
  sop new -s 4 "Roll back a release"

  # Create a new SOP that updates SOP 7, for a case it left out:
  sop new -u 7 "Roll back a database migration"

Flags:
  -h, --help                  help for new
  -s, --supersede REFERENCE   a REFERENCE (number or partial filename) to a record this one supersedes
  -u, --update REFERENCE      a REFERENCE (number or partial filename) to a record this one updates
sop help link
Records, in both SOPs, that SOURCE supersedes or updates TARGET.

SOURCE and TARGET are both a reference (number or partial filename) to an
SOP.  Exactly one of --supersede and --update must be given.

With --supersede, SOURCE is a new procedure for the task TARGET set out, and
TARGET is marked "Superseded by" SOURCE and loses its Draft or Approved
status.

With --update, SOURCE changes part of TARGET, such as a step for a case it
left out, and TARGET is marked "Updated by" SOURCE and keeps its status.

Usage:
  sop link (--supersede | --update) SOURCE TARGET [flags]

Examples:
  # Record that SOP 3 updates SOP 2:
  sop link --update 3 2

  # Record that SOP 5 supersedes SOP 4:
  sop link -s 5 4

Flags:
  -h, --help        help for link
  -s, --supersede   SOURCE supersedes TARGET, which loses its Draft or Approved status
  -u, --update      SOURCE makes substantive changes to TARGET
